  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers, and potentially a few thunderstorms, \nlikely late this weekend into early next week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA cold front approaches our area tonight as an area of low \npressure moves north along it Sunday night. The low should pass \nnorth of our area Sunday night into Monday while strengthening. \nModels have come into better agreement on timing, with Monday \nlooking like the period for the heaviest rainfall but there are \nstill some differences in the guidance that prevent higher \nconfidence in this event. Ensembles show about about a 60-80% \nchance of at least 0.5 inches of rainfall over the North with \nlower chances of 30-40% further south. One thing to watch will \nbe if low pressure can develop over the Gulf of Maine. Models \ngenerally only show weak development with offshore rain bands \nbut if this trends upwards then higher totals may be possible \nalong the coast. Some thunderstorms remain possible Sunday over \nthe North with the advancing cold front and during the day on \nMonday areawide. Currently thinking the greatest chance for \nstorms will be Sunday, especially if clouds are not too \nwidespread. Monday will likely feature isolated elevated \nconvection, mostly away from the coast. Another key factor to \nwatch with this event is fog. Areas of fog, possibly dense, \nexpected tonight and Sunday night as light southerly winds bring\na moist, stable marine layer inland. Expect the densest fog \nsouth of the Katahdin region. Patchy fog may persist into each \nmorning.",
